        <h2>What Is a Blood Test Report?</h2>
        <p>A blood test report — also called a <strong>blood work report</strong> or <strong>pathology report</strong> — is a document generated by a clinical laboratory after analyzing a sample of your blood. When you visit a lab like Thyrocare, SRL Diagnostics, or Apollo Diagnostics, they draw a blood sample, run it through automated analyzers, and produce a report with values for various parameters.</p>
        <p>The most commonly ordered blood test in India is the <strong>Complete Blood Count (CBC)</strong>, also called a <strong>Haemogram</strong>. It gives your doctor a snapshot of your overall health and can reveal conditions like anemia, infection, or nutritional deficiencies.</p>

        <h2>Understanding the Layout of Your Blood Report</h2>
        <p>Every blood report, whether from Thyrocare, Lal PathLabs, or a government hospital, follows a similar structure:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Parameter Name:</strong> The name of what is being measured (e.g., Hemoglobin, WBC)</li>
          <li><strong>Your Value:</strong> The actual measurement from your blood sample</li>
          <li><strong>Units:</strong> How the value is measured (g/dL, cells/µL, etc.)</li>
          <li><strong>Reference Range:</strong> The normal range for that parameter</li>
          <li><strong>Flag:</strong> Marked as "H" (High) or "L" (Low) if outside the normal range</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Complete Blood Count (CBC) Parameters Explained</h2>

        <h3>1. Hemoglobin (HGB or Hb)</h3>
        <p>Hemoglobin is the protein inside red blood cells that carries oxygen from your lungs to the rest of your body. It is one of the most important values in your blood test.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Hemoglobin Ranges (India):</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adult Men: <strong>13.0 – 17.5 g/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Adult Women: <strong>11.5 – 15.5 g/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Pregnant Women: <strong>≥ 11.0 g/dL</strong> (WHO threshold)</li>
            <li>Children (6-12 yrs): <strong>11.5 – 15.5 g/dL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Low Hemoglobin (Anemia):</strong> If your HGB is below the lower limit, it may indicate anemia — a very common condition in India, especially in women. Causes include iron deficiency, B12 deficiency, or chronic disease. Symptoms include fatigue, pale skin, and shortness of breath.</p>
        <p><strong>High Hemoglobin:</strong> Can indicate dehydration, high-altitude living, or lung disease (like COPD). Smokers often have slightly elevated values.</p>

        <h3>2. RBC (Red Blood Cell Count)</h3>
        <p>RBC count measures the actual number of red blood cells in a cubic millimeter of blood.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal RBC Ranges (India):</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Men: <strong>4.5 – 5.9 million/µL</strong></li>
            <li>Women: <strong>4.0 – 5.2 million/µL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p>Low RBC (along with low Hb) confirms anemia. High RBC (Polycythemia) can suggest dehydration or lung conditions.</p>

        <h3>3. WBC (White Blood Cell Count)</h3>
        <p>WBC, also called the Leukocyte Count, measures your immune cells. White blood cells fight infections and are your body's defense system.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal WBC Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>4,000 – 11,000 cells/µL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>High WBC (Leukocytosis):</strong> Usually indicates active infection (bacterial, viral, or fungal), inflammation, or in rare cases, blood cancers like leukemia. If your WBC is high and you have fever, your doctor may investigate for infection.</p>
        <p><strong>Low WBC (Leukopenia):</strong> Can result from viral infections (like dengue or COVID-19 recovery), bone marrow suppression, or certain medications.</p>

        <h3>4. Platelets (PLT)</h3>
        <p>Platelets are tiny blood cells that help form clots to stop bleeding when you get injured.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Platelet Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>1,50,000 – 4,00,000 /µL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Low Platelets (Thrombocytopenia):</strong> Common during dengue fever in India. Very low counts (below 50,000) can be dangerous and require hospitalization.</p>
        <p><strong>High Platelets (Thrombocytosis):</strong> Can occur after infection, iron deficiency, or inflammatory conditions.</p>

        <h3>5. MCV (Mean Corpuscular Volume)</h3>
        <p>MCV measures the average size of your red blood cells. This is crucial for determining the <em>type</em> of anemia.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal MCV Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li><strong>80 – 100 fL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Low MCV (Microcytic anemia):</strong> Usually indicates iron deficiency or thalassemia trait</li>
          <li><strong>High MCV (Macrocytic anemia):</strong> Usually indicates Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency</li>
          <li><strong>Normal MCV with low Hb (Normocytic anemia):</strong> May indicate chronic disease or early deficiency</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>6. Hematocrit (HCT / PCV)</h3>
        <p>Hematocrit or Packed Cell Volume (PCV) is the percentage of your blood volume that consists of red blood cells.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal HCT Ranges:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Men: <strong>40 – 54%</strong></li>
            <li>Women: <strong>36 – 48%</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>7. Differential WBC Count (Diff Count)</h3>
        <p>The differential count breaks down WBCs into their subtypes. Here are the main ones:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Neutrophils (40–75%):</strong> Fight bacterial infections. High neutrophils = likely bacterial infection.</li>
          <li><strong>Lymphocytes (20–45%):</strong> Fight viral infections. High lymphocytes = likely viral infection.</li>
          <li><strong>Eosinophils (1–6%):</strong> Elevated in allergies, asthma, and parasitic infections.</li>
          <li><strong>Monocytes (2–10%):</strong> Part of the innate immune response.</li>
          <li><strong>Basophils (0–1%):</strong> Involved in allergic reactions.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What Do "H" and "L" Flags Mean?</h2>
        <p>When a value falls outside the reference range, labs automatically flag it:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>"H" or "↑"</strong> = High — the value is above the upper limit of normal</li>
          <li><strong>"L" or "↓"</strong> = Low — the value is below the lower limit of normal</li>
          <li><strong>"*" or "!"</strong> = Critical — the value is significantly outside range and may need urgent attention</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Importantly, a single "H" or "L" flag does not automatically mean something is wrong. Reference ranges are statistical ranges — roughly 5% of healthy people will have a value flagged purely by chance. Context, your symptoms, and other test values all matter.</p>

        <h2>Common Reasons for Abnormal Blood Test Results in India</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Iron Deficiency Anemia:</strong> Extremely common, especially in women and vegetarians. Low Hb, low MCV, low Serum Ferritin.</li>
          <li><strong>Vitamin B12 Deficiency:</strong> Very prevalent in vegetarians and vegans. High MCV, low RBC.</li>
          <li><strong>Dengue Fever:</strong> Causes low WBC and severely low Platelets.</li>
          <li><strong>Typhoid / Bacterial Infection:</strong> Elevated WBC with high Neutrophils.</li>
          <li><strong>Dehydration:</strong> Can cause falsely elevated RBC, Hb, and Hematocrit.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What Should You Do After Getting Your Blood Report?</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Don't panic</strong> — a few flagged values are common and not always serious.</li>
          <li><strong>Upload your report to HealthSaral</strong> — get every marker explained in plain language instantly.</li>
          <li><strong>Note your symptoms</strong> — the most valuable context your doctor needs.</li>
          <li><strong>See your doctor</strong> — especially if multiple values are flagged or you feel unwell.</li>
          <li><strong>Don't self-medicate</strong> — based on blood reports alone.</li>
        </ol>

